1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
You handed out evaluations?

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:02,740
Yes.

3
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:07,480
Yeah, I'm going to travel through time and then something.

4
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:09,400
OK.

5
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:10,280
All right.

6
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:12,440
So today we're going to finish up with labor,

7
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:14,720
and then we're going to do financial markets,

8
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,600
and then after that, let's see.

9
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,000
Then next week, we're going to do public choice,

10
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,960
and then we're done.

11
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:23,520
So let's get started.

12
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:25,600
Could you close the door in the back there, Elaine?

13
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:26,720
Thanks a lot.

14
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:30,960
All right, so two more topics for labor economics.

15
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:34,080
All right, first one, nominal rigidities, often discussed.

16
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:36,280
At the beginning of the fall semester,

17
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:39,160
we had Truman Buley out to give a seminar.

18
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:41,440
It was very hard to hear him for some reason,

19
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:43,560
but he said he had a constant volume.

20
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:44,640
He can't raise his volume.

21
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:46,160
But anyway, the book is really good.

22
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:53,000
So long noted, probably for several centuries,

23
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,120
that there's something funny about labor markets,

24
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:57,000
because even though labor markets seem

25
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,920
very close to the perfectly competitive assumptions,

26
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:02,800
but it's very unusual for nominal wages to fall

27
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,440
when there's surplus labor.

28
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:06,840
So during periods of very high employment,

29
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:09,000
in a market that is close to perfectly competitive,

30
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:10,480
there's an obvious thing to expect,

31
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,300
which is just that the price will crash,

32
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:17,160
restoring full employment or ending the surplus.

33
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:20,520
But this generally does not seem to happen in labor markets.

34
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:23,040
You can always look and find some way to blame government

35
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:25,040
and say government is hindering adjustment,

36
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:27,920
but normally when government is trying to hinder adjustment,

37
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,760
the market is trying to hinder the hindering

38
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:31,440
of the adjustment.

39
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:33,920
That's the normal thing, is that there's creative evasion

40
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:34,480
in business.

41
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:36,680
When government tells them they have to do something,

42
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:38,080
oh yeah, we have to do that.

43
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:40,880
OK, well, how can we not actually do that,

44
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:43,080
even though you say you have to and then make money?

45
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:44,960
But for nominal virginity, it seems

46
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:48,000
like it's a very stable thing that not that much effort

47
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,320
is used to, or not that much energy trying to evade it.

48
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:55,240
Now, there is a simple neoclassical theory

49
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:59,920
of nominal virginity, and that is just menu costs.

50
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,120
There is a neoclassical theory to explain it.

51
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:03,480
This is menu costs.

52
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:05,040
This is a simple neoclassical theory

53
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:07,320
about why, say, restaurants don't adjust the prices

54
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:08,400
on their menu every single day.

55
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:11,200
Well, they don't want to incur the expense of printing out

56
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,840
new menus with the daily prices.

57
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:16,440
And therefore, if you print out menus

58
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:19,120
where the prices that are going to last for a year or two

59
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:21,680
years, then on any given day, the market price

60
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:23,520
won't be quite right.

61
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,360
And then, of course, firms make a trade-off

62
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:27,840
between how often to adjust the menu

63
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:31,040
and how off the prices are going to be.

64
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:33,600
And sometimes you can see that restaurants will have

65
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:36,120
a few special items that fluctuate widely in price

66
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,040
daily, like lobster, where they don't put

67
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:39,040
the price on the menu.

68
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:40,160
They say market price.

69
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:42,200
And once you learn some economics, you might say,

70
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:43,560
wait, it's all market prices.

71
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:44,720
Everything's a market price.

72
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:46,260
What do you mean is the market price?

73
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:48,640
Really, what they mean is the daily price,

74
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:49,440
something like that.

75
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:53,040
So it's all market prices, but some market prices last a year

76
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:56,000
and others only last a day.

77
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,840
So that is an alternative explanation.

78
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:02,560
But in general, it seems like the menu costs and labor

79
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:04,840
are pretty small relative to the value of the product.

80
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:06,840
Furthermore, menu costs, the menu cost model

81
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:08,760
has a simple implication, which is

82
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:10,960
that the bigger the shock to demand,

83
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:13,360
the smaller the problem will be.

84
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:16,440
So the idea with menu costs is there might be a 1% change,

85
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:19,480
and then it's not worth changing the menu over 1% change.

86
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:21,680
But what do we see during the Great Depression?

87
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:24,440
You have a 1 third fall in the money supply,

88
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:28,120
and therefore wages adjust instantly or very quickly.

89
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:30,040
So well, by God, there's a huge fall in demand.

90
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:32,640
We've got now the menu costs are irrelevant compared

91
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:36,080
to all the losses of keeping a quarter of workers unemployed.

92
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:38,460
But that's not what happened during the Great Depression.

93
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:40,720
Instead, you had many years of high unemployment

94
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:42,520
after this huge shock.

95
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:44,720
So the menu cost model says really the problems

96
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:47,960
come from tiny shocks, shocks that are so small it's not

97
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:50,120
worth changing the menu.

98
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:54,280
But in later markets, we seem to see that very large shocks

99
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:56,560
have large effects.

100
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,160
Whereas in a simple menu cost model,

101
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:01,960
there would be a small range where, of course,

102
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:04,440
a double microscopic shock will have more effect

103
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:05,840
than a microscopic shock.

104
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:07,520
But once you get over the threshold

105
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:09,280
where it's worth printing up another menu,

106
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:10,760
then the problem should be solved,

107
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:13,720
and it doesn't look like that in the real world.

108
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:16,880
So in light of all this, a lot of behavioral economists

109
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:18,440
have come up with alternative stories

110
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:20,960
of what's really going wrong in labor markets.

111
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:24,120
And the two big stories are, first of all, money illusion.

112
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:31,840
And second of all, some kind of nominal fairness norm.

113
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:36,560
So for one thing, there are a bunch

114
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,020
of studies that indicate people have money illusion to some

115
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:39,760
degree.

116
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:41,840
That means that when there's low inflation,

117
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:44,800
people tend to think of the value of money as constant

118
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:47,160
rather than slightly changing.

119
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:50,760
So how often have you gone to the same store

120
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:52,600
for a few weeks in a row, and each time you

121
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:54,720
see the price is the same, and each time you say,

122
00:04:54,720 --> 00:05:00,280
oh, the price is the inflation rate divided by 52% lower?

123
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:02,800
That's not the normal reaction.

124
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:04,520
As long as there's positive inflation,

125
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:06,140
every week they don't raise the price,

126
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:09,320
they've really lowered the real price.

127
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:10,960
But that's your story.

128
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:11,720
Maybe you do that.

129
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:13,160
Maybe you do do that, in which case

130
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:15,520
you're in the right business.

131
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:18,000
Then you're really internalizing economic norms,

132
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,520
like just three more weeks, and then it

133
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:22,960
will be worth buying this.

134
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:24,680
But that's one story.

135
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:29,640
Now, what's striking is, though, even when you go and make

136
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:33,240
the point very explicit, so it's very hard to really still

137
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:34,920
have money illusion, people still

138
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:37,960
seem to actually define fairness in nominal terms.

139
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:40,200
Even when you show them, look, it's

140
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:42,960
totally the same in real terms.

141
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:45,360
Well, maybe totally the same economically,

142
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:49,080
but not fairness-wise, where something different's going on.

143
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:52,080
So here is a famous behavioral study.

144
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:53,640
People were given two hypotheticals,

145
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:55,180
and then you randomly assign the two,

146
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:57,480
and they're out, and you're asked to rate the fairness.

147
00:05:57,480 --> 00:05:58,760
So two hypotheticals.

148
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:01,200
One, inflation is zero, and then you give everybody

149
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:03,680
a nominal 7% pay cut.

150
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:05,960
And what percent of people consider that unfair?

151
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:07,360
62%.

152
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:09,240
What if inflation is 12%, and you only

153
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:11,160
give people a 5% nominal raise?

154
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:13,680
Then only 22% consider that unfair.

155
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:14,960
And here, you're very explicit.

156
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:15,800
Here's the pay cut.

157
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:18,040
Here's the inflation.

158
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:19,640
See how it's the same thing?

159
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:23,800
And people don't actually feel like it's the same thing.

160
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:26,920
Now, you may respond, well, who cares about fairness anyway?

161
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:27,840
This is a business.

162
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:30,040
It's not like a fairness factory.

163
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:34,400
Well, there's also evidence that disgruntled workers' pay is

164
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:34,900
worse.

165
00:06:34,900 --> 00:06:38,920
Or, excuse me, disgruntled workers' performance is worse.

166
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:41,160
Productivity falls when workers feel like they're

167
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:42,920
being badly treated.

168
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:45,160
And one of the big lessons of Truman Buley's book,

169
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:47,680
Why Wages Don't Fall During a Recession,

170
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:49,520
he went around and did hundreds of interviews

171
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:51,120
with hundreds of different firms.

172
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:54,240
Standard result is nominal pay cuts seem unfair to workers.

173
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:55,320
Who cares?

174
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:57,360
Well, I'd better care because they're my workers.

175
00:06:57,360 --> 00:06:59,080
And when my workers feel unfairly treated,

176
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:02,120
they don't do a good job anymore.

177
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:04,700
All right, so in other words, effort's partly about incentive,

178
00:07:04,700 --> 00:07:05,540
partly about trust.

179
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:07,800
So if people feel like you have screwed them over,

180
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,320
then performance falls.

181
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:12,600
And you may actually prefer paying people higher wage

182
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,400
as long as the productivity is proportionately higher.

183
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:17,600
On the other hand, you may not want to go and cut pay

184
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:19,880
because you might cut pay by 5%, but if productivity

185
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:25,080
falls by 10%, you actually had a bad deal out of this.

186
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:27,360
So Buley spent a lot of time asking employers,

187
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:30,200
how do workers respond to wage cuts versus layoffs?

188
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:33,160
Very standard reaction is wage cuts hurt productivity,

189
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:36,680
whereas layoffs actually improve productivity.

190
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:40,240
And again, usual story is that when there are layoffs,

191
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:43,760
the remaining workers work harder because they're scared.

192
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:45,480
Of course, there's also a selection effect,

193
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:46,960
but this is supposedly in addition

194
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:48,000
to the selection effect.

195
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,360
When you say selection effect, you mean?

196
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:51,800
That you would lay off the worst workers.

197
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:52,300
Exactly.

198
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:52,960
Yeah.

199
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:54,800
Yes, but again, the story that people

200
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:58,640
with firsthand knowledge on both sides of the employment

201
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:01,360
relation will say is when there are layoffs,

202
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:03,480
well, employers say the workers seem to work harder

203
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:05,320
because they're scared, and the workers say,

204
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:07,320
well, I work harder because I'm scared.

205
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,520
I don't want to lose this job.

206
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:09,440
I really need this job.

207
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:11,440
I don't want to get unemployed in this recession.

208
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:14,680
I just need to make it through this next round of layoffs,

209
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:16,720
and I've got to make sure people know my value

210
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:19,560
so I don't get laid off.

211
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:24,040
My favorite real world example of this, so in the early 1990s,

212
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:27,920
the state of California had an especially bad budget crunch.

213
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:30,200
And I was told secondhand by someone

214
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:34,160
who was in the faculty lounge at the Berkeley Economics

215
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:36,560
Department that there were theorists sitting around

216
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:39,040
complaining about their nominal pay cut.

217
00:08:39,040 --> 00:08:40,920
All right, now that might say, well, of course,

218
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:42,420
they don't like getting a nominal pay cut.

219
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:44,320
The same observer said that 10 years earlier,

220
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,800
when the real pay cut was way higher.

221
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:49,400
Because remember, early 80s, think about how high inflation

222
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:50,240
was.

223
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:53,760
So even if you're getting a substantial nominal raise,

224
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:57,240
still, the real wages are getting cut very rapidly.

225
00:08:57,240 --> 00:09:01,040
But my same inside observer said, now, the same theorists,

226
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:04,680
these are PhD theorists with tenure at Berkeley,

227
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:08,920
were complaining a lot more during a period of no raises,

228
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:13,160
or I guess it was like very mild nominal pay cuts

229
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:17,400
and near zero inflation, than they were 10, 15 years earlier

230
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:19,820
when they were getting moderate nominal raises

231
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:22,360
and there was high inflation.

232
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:27,520
So even they seemed to actually be vocally more upset and more

233
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:33,000
outraged by the high inflation, moderate nominal raises regime,

234
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,720
which means substantial real pay cuts,

235
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:39,240
than they were during the minimal inflation

236
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:42,040
and absolute nominal wage cuts, which were actually not

237
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:43,840
as bad as the earlier ones for them.

238
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:45,160
Yeah, Ash?

239
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:49,000
On that point about how workers respond to layoffs, et cetera,

240
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:54,080
Tyler Cowen said that one way to view how productivity increased

241
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:56,240
during the Great Recession after layoffs

242
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:57,680
is that the people who were laid off

243
00:09:57,680 --> 00:09:59,080
and zero marginal productivity.

244
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:00,840
Yeah, so that is a standard story, right?

245
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:02,720
And I was saying, so the selection alone would give this

246
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:06,400
to you, and this is why if you believe that people

247
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:07,880
who have a lot of first-hand experience

248
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:10,040
with an industry know something about it,

249
00:10:10,040 --> 00:10:11,340
they're saying something on top of that.

250
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:13,720
They're actually saying that the one and the same worker

251
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:16,200
who remains works harder from the fear of the layoffs.

252
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:18,560
But yes, there is Tyler's story,

253
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:20,600
which is just an extreme version of an earlier story

254
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:25,120
of you lay off the lower productivity people, right,

255
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:28,320
as to what Tyler's evidence ever was for their zero product.

256
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:30,680
I don't know what that, I've asked him many times.

257
00:10:30,680 --> 00:10:32,680
I've never gotten an answer better than, well,

258
00:10:32,680 --> 00:10:37,440
there's some selection, but it's hardly the same as zero.

259
00:10:37,440 --> 00:10:38,720
Yeah.

260
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:44,200
Yes. Right, there are some, right?

261
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:49,400
Yeah. So, I mean, it would be surprising if there were a lot

262
00:10:49,400 --> 00:10:52,320
of zeros but no negatives, right?

263
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:53,800
Just by statistics.

264
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:56,320
So, like, whatever, there's like this giant spike at zero,

265
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:58,240
that must be some miracle that there's a bunch of people

266
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:01,840
who do, on balance, do nothing, exactly.

267
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:05,120
Right? So, but, yeah.

268
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:08,400
Okay. Now, the classic response to nominal rigidities,

269
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:12,360
of course, which we know from Keynes as well as people before

270
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:16,680
and after, is just to inflate away the nominal rigidities.

271
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:19,520
So, if you've got a problem where you have 25%

272
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:20,720
of your workforce unemployed,

273
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:22,520
and you've got almost no inflation,

274
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:25,160
why don't we just print up a ton of money, cause a bunch

275
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:29,720
of inflation, and hope the workers are too dumb to notice?

276
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:31,760
Right? Or maybe they, or maybe they're,

277
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:34,040
maybe they're even aware, but then they don't feel

278
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:35,640
like it's unfair.

279
00:11:35,640 --> 00:11:36,960
Or maybe they feel like it's unfair,

280
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:38,480
and they think it's the government being unfair rather

281
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:41,040
than employers, because there's also plenty of evidence

282
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:46,320
that people blame employers for pay, but government for inflation.

283
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:48,640
So, in which case, you could deflect the blame,

284
00:11:48,640 --> 00:11:51,480
and the government can sort of throw itself on the grenade

285
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:54,880
and say, yes, hate us, don't hate your employers.

286
00:11:54,880 --> 00:11:57,400
And then, presumably, workers will not try to get revenge

287
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:00,840
on their employers by stealing or doing a bad job

288
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:04,240
or being rude to customers or just slowing down or whatever.

289
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:05,880
Right. Now, in practice, as you probably know,

290
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:08,040
just inflating away nominal wage rigidities is easier

291
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:09,200
said than done.

292
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:11,080
Lots of countries have tried.

293
00:12:11,080 --> 00:12:13,760
Countries that do it a lot seem to be really bad at it.

294
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:17,760
There's a paper by Robert Lucas in the 70s, the punchline

295
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:20,560
of which is that the higher your long-run rate

296
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:24,120
of nominal GDP growth, the worse the benefit of inflation is

297
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:25,600
for employment and output.

298
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:27,240
So, countries where they have a long history

299
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:31,680
of very high demand growth and very high inflation,

300
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:34,400
in those countries, Keynesian policies don't work anymore.

301
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:36,040
Whereas, countries that generally have almost no

302
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:37,280
inflation, in those countries,

303
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:39,240
Keynesian policies do seem to work.

304
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:41,040
But then, there's the problem of once you say, hey,

305
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:43,320
Keynesian policies work really great in a country like ours,

306
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:45,120
let's use them all the time.

307
00:12:45,120 --> 00:12:47,120
Well, if that's your reaction, then before long,

308
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:48,960
you don't remain one of those countries with low inflation.

309
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:51,240
You turn into one of the countries

310
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:53,760
where the policies don't work.

311
00:12:53,760 --> 00:12:54,120
All right.

312
00:12:54,120 --> 00:12:54,920
So, yes?

313
00:12:54,920 --> 00:12:57,320
So, in a world of digital menus,

314
00:12:57,320 --> 00:13:01,360
do menu cost models still have any validity?

315
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:02,200
Or are they still?

316
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:03,880
Well, so as long as there's that, you know,

317
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:05,720
like so you could take it up a level and say

318
00:13:05,720 --> 00:13:07,800
that rewriting the algorithm for the,

319
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:09,560
rewriting the pricing algorithm,

320
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:13,480
that might actually change if market conditions change.

321
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:14,800
Of course, if you have the perfect algorithm,

322
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:16,080
you never need to change it.

323
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:17,760
But from what I understand in real world programming,

324
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:19,000
algorithms change all the time.

325
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:20,480
People are always improving on them,

326
00:13:20,480 --> 00:13:22,040
and they never hit the perfect one.

327
00:13:22,040 --> 00:13:25,200
So, then you might actually just get a, you know,

328
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:27,600
like you might get a change in the algorithm,

329
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:29,280
or people might put more effort in the algorithm,

330
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:33,080
or you might have more variable, like a more dynamic algorithm.

331
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:34,240
So, there's still that.

332
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:36,600
I do know that during the 2008 recession,

333
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:39,840
multiple economists told me that they subscribed

334
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:45,720
to just a menu cost view of why you have nominal wage rigidity.

335
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:48,400
And so, they were predicting that better inventory systems

336
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:50,640
and so on would mean that whatever unemployment happened

337
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:52,280
would be very short-lived.

338
00:13:52,280 --> 00:13:54,400
And I said, well, I believe the psychological theory.

339
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:56,720
I don't think the menu cost really explains very much.

340
00:13:56,720 --> 00:14:00,480
And I don't like to be, I don't like to be claimed

341
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:03,920
to be proven right by offense, but that's in the ballpark

342
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:05,240
of being proven right by offense.

343
00:14:05,240 --> 00:14:07,840
It did take just as long, roughly,

344
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:10,520
for the Great Recession to go away as previous times.

345
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:14,440
So, a good long-run rule is that after inflation, or rather,

346
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:16,880
after unemployment reaches its peak, it declines

347
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:20,080
by one percentage point per year until you go back to normal.

348
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:23,320
And if you take a look at the graph for the Great Recession,

349
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:26,000
it almost perfectly is on that line.

350
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,320
So, there is the question, well, how high up will you go?

351
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:30,440
And that's, I don't know any good rule for that.

352
00:14:30,440 --> 00:14:34,000
But once you hit the peak, once it starts, once it stops growing,

353
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,840
then the percentage point per year rule works really great

354
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:39,800
for the last 70 years, right?

355
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:41,680
Which, if you think about it, it actually is a little bit

356
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:43,480
of a challenge for almost every theory

357
00:14:43,480 --> 00:14:48,440
because, wait a second, so, doesn't the quality

358
00:14:48,440 --> 00:14:52,960
of contracyclical policy vary over time in some way?

359
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:55,920
Well, if it does, then how come there is this very clear rule

360
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:59,520
of one percentage point per year that unemployment goes back

361
00:14:59,520 --> 00:15:01,160
down after recession, so wasn't there, like,

362
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:02,960
a better time and a worse time?

363
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:04,520
So, I guess the Great Depression does not fit it.

364
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:07,560
So, you might say macro policy was noticeably worse during the

365
00:15:07,560 --> 00:15:10,240
Great Depression, but everything post-war seems to be

366
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:12,320
on that line pretty closely.

367
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:14,160
So, I encourage you, if you're curious, to go

368
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:16,200
and actually look at the BLS data.

369
00:15:16,200 --> 00:15:17,800
So, I mean, I've looked at that.

370
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:19,360
And, you know, on the basis of that,

371
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:21,960
I made this totally atheoretical bet with Tyler

372
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:24,840
that unemployment wouldn't remain above 5%

373
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:26,720
for 20 years, as he predicted.

374
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:29,080
He gave me 10 to 1 odds as well and lost in three years.

375
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:32,480
So, there, vindication by bet.

376
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:35,680
Now, that's real vindication, right, because that's one

377
00:15:35,680 --> 00:15:38,800
where both sides specified in advance what they would count

378
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,560
as being right and wrong.

379
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:43,280
So, it's easy to wait around to see what happens and say, see,

380
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:45,600
that was pretty much what I was talking about.

381
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:48,600
Another thing when it's well-defined publicly

382
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:51,200
where you can actually see that someone was,

383
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:53,240
in fact, definitely right.

384
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:55,080
Okay. Now, last topic.

385
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:55,920
Another fun one.

386
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:57,520
Efficiency wages.

387
00:15:57,520 --> 00:15:57,760
All right.

388
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:01,560
So, you've all heard about how garbage men get extra money.

389
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:03,920
That's a basic fact about the economy.

390
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:05,240
Most important fact to explain,

391
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:07,440
why garbage men get to make more money, right?

392
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:12,120
And, yeah, so if this is supply and demand for normal workers

393
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:15,120
with the skills of garbage men, then what is different

394
00:16:15,120 --> 00:16:18,160
about the market for garbage collection?

395
00:16:18,160 --> 00:16:23,200
Yes, yes, and therefore, what curve shifts and how?

396
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:26,000
Supply, yes, supply is lower.

397
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:27,360
A lot of people want to say demand goes

398
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:29,040
up because you have to pay workers more in order

399
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:31,000
to deter the unpleasant conditions, but the fact

400
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,440
that a job is miserable doesn't make it any more profitable

401
00:16:33,440 --> 00:16:35,200
to hire people, right?

402
00:16:35,200 --> 00:16:37,520
So, remember, demand is based upon worker productivity,

403
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:39,640
so the fact that you're miserable doesn't make you

404
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:41,600
more productive, right?

405
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:43,680
So, what really happens is the people don't want to take

406
00:16:43,680 --> 00:16:47,400
that job, and therefore, wages go up as a result

407
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:50,480
of reduced supply, and then, of course, employers have to go

408
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:52,240
and pay the higher wage in order to get workers

409
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:54,200
to do this unpleasant job.

410
00:16:54,200 --> 00:16:54,720
All right.

411
00:16:54,720 --> 00:16:57,440
So, this is known, of course, as compensating differentials

412
00:16:57,440 --> 00:16:59,720
or also equalizing differences.

413
00:16:59,720 --> 00:17:01,840
Should be well familiar to you.

414
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:02,760
All right.

415
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:04,080
So, well known.

416
00:17:04,080 --> 00:17:07,520
And notice, is this a model of asymmetric information

417
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:10,040
where people, where the two sides,

418
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:12,080
where one side knows whether the job's unpleasant

419
00:17:12,080 --> 00:17:12,800
or the other one doesn't?

420
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:14,760
Or is it a model of symmetric information

421
00:17:14,760 --> 00:17:17,320
where it's known to both sides what's going on?

422
00:17:17,320 --> 00:17:19,000
Yeah. What's that?

423
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:20,000
Yeah, it's symmetric.

424
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,160
So, you know, the workers know how it's unpleasant,

425
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:24,760
but employers don't say, gee,

426
00:17:24,760 --> 00:17:26,720
I don't wonder whether a garbage collection is unpleasant

427
00:17:26,720 --> 00:17:27,360
or not.

428
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:28,320
Who knows?

429
00:17:28,320 --> 00:17:30,200
How would one discover such things?

430
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:32,720
Instead, it's just common knowledge, and of course,

431
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:34,600
for a job like that, we're going to debate more.

432
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:36,200
And workers know, of course, for a job like that,

433
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:37,560
you're going to debate me more.

434
00:17:37,560 --> 00:17:37,720
All right.

435
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:39,440
No big surprise.

436
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:40,200
All right.

437
00:17:40,200 --> 00:17:43,480
Now, what about in a market with asymmetric information?

438
00:17:43,480 --> 00:17:45,240
Market with asymmetric information

439
00:17:45,240 --> 00:17:47,000
where suppose workers know more

440
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,080
about whether they're doing unpleasant stuff than you do?

441
00:17:50,080 --> 00:17:52,400
Like one thing that's generally considered unpleasant is having

442
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:55,560
to go and work really hard all the time.

443
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:58,640
So, if you could either have a leisurely pace

444
00:17:58,640 --> 00:18:00,800
or you could have a frantic pace.

445
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:03,400
Most people don't like having a frantic pace.

446
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:06,680
So, if you ever try to get someone to hurry up

447
00:18:06,680 --> 00:18:08,080
and notice the resentment that a lot

448
00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:10,080
of people feel at being hurried.

449
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:12,920
I'm a non-coffee drinker, so I especially notice the coffee

450
00:18:12,920 --> 00:18:16,240
drinkers get very agitated when you try to speed them along.

451
00:18:16,240 --> 00:18:17,360
Can we go now?

452
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:20,400
I'm drinking coffee.

453
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:23,960
So, I never say I can't hurry because I'm drinking a glass

454
00:18:23,960 --> 00:18:26,960
of water, so usually you can't hurry up because you have a

455
00:18:26,960 --> 00:18:28,360
different beverage than me.

456
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:33,600
So, great anger at trying to hurry a coffee drinker.

457
00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:36,680
It's an attack upon them as human beings

458
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:38,680
to try to move them along.

459
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:42,440
Anyway, so what if we've got asymmetric information

460
00:18:42,440 --> 00:18:45,560
where the boss wants you to go and work fast,

461
00:18:45,560 --> 00:18:46,720
but you don't really feel like it

462
00:18:46,720 --> 00:18:51,120
and the boss has trouble telling how fast you're actually working?

463
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:52,920
Well, in that case, what might you do as an employer?

464
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:56,480
You might say work fast or I will fire you,

465
00:18:56,480 --> 00:18:59,520
but there's a little problem here, which is

466
00:18:59,520 --> 00:19:01,920
that if the market's competitive,

467
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:05,160
the threat to fire someone is not scary.

468
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:08,800
Just like if there's 100 sellers of strawberries

469
00:19:08,800 --> 00:19:11,160
and one seller of strawberries says,

470
00:19:11,160 --> 00:19:13,440
I don't like the way you're touching my strawberries.

471
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:15,520
You can never buy a strawberry here again.

472
00:19:15,520 --> 00:19:19,720
It's like, fine, walk three feet and buy it from another guy.

473
00:19:19,720 --> 00:19:22,200
All right, so it's not very scary to ban someone

474
00:19:22,200 --> 00:19:25,000
from your store if there's lots of other stores that are just

475
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:26,760
as good and very close by.

476
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:29,640
Similarly, if it's really easy to get another job that's just

477
00:19:29,640 --> 00:19:32,800
as good with the same pay, then it's not very scary

478
00:19:32,800 --> 00:19:35,320
to threaten to fire somebody.

479
00:19:35,320 --> 00:19:37,160
And that is the problem.

480
00:19:37,160 --> 00:19:39,440
All right, so if you are trying to get someone

481
00:19:39,440 --> 00:19:41,200
to work really hard and you say, look, I'm hiring you.

482
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:42,720
I expect you to work really hard.

483
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:44,240
I know I can't monitor you all the time,

484
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:49,520
but if I ever catch you doing things slowly, I will fire you.

485
00:19:49,520 --> 00:19:51,600
And then the person, oh, no, I would never do that.

486
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:54,760
And then if you happen to be caught by your boss

487
00:19:54,760 --> 00:19:57,320
and he fires you, all right, well, just move over

488
00:19:57,320 --> 00:19:58,320
to somebody else.

489
00:19:58,320 --> 00:20:25,320
Get the same deal you were offering.


